<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meilyr Brydydd</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/1100">1100</a>–<a href="/years/1137">1137</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Meilyr Brydydd was a Welsh court poet, the earliest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poets-of-the-princes">Poets of the Princes</a> whose work survives. He served Gruffudd ap Cynan, king of Gwynedd, and wrote an elegy on his death in 1137, as well as a deathbed poem asking to be buried on Bardsey Island. His son Gwalchmai and grandsons continued the family's tradition of court poetry.</p></div>
